Z||000000||Bandit2_0||08-31-2000||10:04 PM||||  My Aunt wants to hook her turntable to her computer so she can record songs on to a CD.  Now my question is, is she going to need a preamp?  I have never tried this so I have no clue.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>He's the kind of guy that would drink a gallon of gasoline just so he could piss on your campfire.||||reg||12
Z||000001||fierospeeder||09-01-2000||12:30 AM||||im just guessing, but if the player has like head phones, you can get a male to male plug that can go in your microphone jack on your soundcard,<BR>some newer players have rca jacks and that might be able to get plugged into sound cards<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||09-01-2000||09:22 AM||||Not sure if the "line in" jack has enough sensitivity to pick up a turntable.<BR>Before you buy a preamp, try the "mic in" jack. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||whamel||09-01-2000||09:49 AM||||I tried this a while back to see if I could get some of my records down to CD and found that the quality of the records (and my record player) were so sub par that it sounded terrible once I got it to CD.  I don't know if you have a big library near you, but I ended up going to the library and borrowing the CD's to match my albums and just doing it that way.  It doesn't come off scratchy, there was no line loss from my 'adapter', and I saved myself a LOT of effort and time cleaning up WAV files and converting them to MP3's.<BR>The tough ones I just found off the Internet and downloaded.  Unless it's something really obscure, you shouldn't have too much trouble finding it.  Hmmm, actually - sometimes it is even easier to find the obscure stuff.<BR>Wade<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||09-01-2000||10:24 AM||||you can also do <A HREF="http://www.napster.com" TARGET=_blank>www.napster.com</A> <BR>and download the music<P>when recording music, microsoft has different ways in which it is recorded.<BR>from 8bit to 32 i believe.  You can select the CD quality recording and it takes a lot of harddrive space per second instead of the 8 bit.||||reg||1
Z||000005||whamel||09-01-2000||10:32 AM||||Is Napster still up and running?  I haven't bothered to load it up in a long, long time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-01-2000||12:54 PM||||You can't feed a turntable to a mic jack on most sound cards.... it's not a stereo jack. Mono in wiht power and ground for "condenser" mikes.<P>Line in will only work for a turntable with a ceramic pickup. You'll need a preamp for a magnetic pickup.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||09-01-2000||03:18 PM||||Just plug the turntable in your amplifier and plug the aux out of the amplifier into your soundcard. If your amplifier doesn't have an aux out, then use the tape out.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bandit2_0||09-02-2000||09:48 PM||||Thanks to all who helped.  It turned out that my uncle just ended up pluging the turntable into the back of the soundcard.  He had to turn the volume all the way up to hear a conversation level volume of music.  So he preamped it.  It's working fine now after my aunt got the right software update.<P>Bandit<P><P>------------------<BR>He's the kind of guy that would drink a gallon of gas just so he could piss on your campfire.||||reg||14
